  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 AM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nA shortwave producing thunderstorms over IA early this morning will \nmove eastward into Illinois this morning, with scattered showers and \nthunderstorms likely near and north of Peoria, and a slight chance \nto just north of I-72. A low level jet and warm advection fueling \nthis system has resulted in several storms producing 1 to 1.5 inch \nhail in IA, although 00Z ensemble forecast guidance suggests \ninstability should diminish through the night before it arrives in \nparts of central IL, and we will likely see less intense \nthunderstorm activity. This system should lift to the northeast this \nafternoon, with precipitation ending across central IL by around \nnoon. Rainfall amounts will typically be under a quarter inch, but \nheavier storms could drop isolated amounts over an inch.\n\nThe next precipitation feature of note will be from the remnants of \nHurricane Isaias. Current track forecasts show this system will take \na hard eastward turn south of the Ohio River, leaving dim prospects \nof getting significant rainfall in parts of central or southeast IL.\n12Z ensemble forecast shows only a 10 to 30 percent chance of \nover a half inch south of I-70, and only a few percent chance \nfor over an inch. North of I-70, although there is around a 30 \npercent or higher chance of measurable rain near and east of \nI-57, chances of anything over a quarter inch are generally only\n10 to 20 percent.\n\nA shortwave ridge behind Isaias will bring dry conditions from \nSunday afternoon through Monday before a cold front trailing a \nshortwave passing by to the north brings a slight chance for \nshowers and thunderstorms late Monday night into Tuesday. The \npost frontal airmass will remain conducive for rainfall with \n30-50 pct PoPs expanding across the area Wednesday. A warm front\narriving from the west on Thursday morning will further \nincrease rain chances.\n\nTemperatures will be unseasonably warm today with southerly flow and \nclearing skies bringing highs well into the 80s, which could \nrival records for a few locations. Temperatures will be down a \nfew to several degrees Saturday and Sunday as cloudy conditions \nand a slightly cooler air mass associated with Isaias spread \ninto the area. Monday will feature a quick surge of warm air \nahead of the Monday night's cold front, as highs reach near \nrecord highs in the mid 80s to around 90. Following the cold \nfront, temperatures should be down a few to several degrees, out\nof record territory, but still well above normal. The first \nchance for near or below normal temperatures should hold off \nuntil Friday and beyond behind a late week cold front.",
